[36m(main_task pid=2937563)[0m Doc 4(Title: Oyster) A category is a modeling abstraction, that allows elements to be related to each other. When trying to analyze how applications work in terms of software architecture, considering only the basic software elements (like classes, packages, processes, etc) can be restrictive since the interactions among them are crucial to understand the application and how different components of an application relate to each other. Categories capture a high-level knowledge about the program, without the details of the implementation. For example, for studying the DRY, the categories “processes” and “classes” should be considered relevant; but the categories “readers” and “writers” are irrelevant because they just represent details of the system’s external interface. Processes A process can be defined as a set of actors (processes and/or classes) and data flows. The process can be seen as an information flow direction, from one set of elements (sender processes and/or classes) to another set (receiver processes and/or classes). Processes are mutually referenced, i.e., if one process is a receiver for some data flows, then it sends at least one data flow to other processes. Processes are not related to a particular physical resource (e.g. file or OS process), but to an abstract, functional representation of a computational task. In other words, a process could be seen as the instantiation of a state machine model, where every state transition is represented by a data flow. When classes do not represent the computational tasks directly and they are instead part of some larger computation task, those classes are part of a process represented by another element. A process is then a logical abstraction of a data flow model (i.e. input and output data flows and method invocations). In general, a data flow can
