[36m(main_task pid=2937563)[0m In 2005 Australian workers had a median total working life entitlement of 15 weeks. The system varies greatly between states. For example, the system in Tasmania has just one long service leave award, which entitles employees to two weeks off for every year of continuous employment with the employer. The Victorian system offers a fortnight off every two years of continuous employment. It also treats part-time employees differently, entitling them to a fortnight off for every four years of continuous employment. The New South Wales system treats part-timers similarly to full-timers. In South Australia, each year of continuous employment beyond 52 weeks entitles the employee to two weeks of paid long service leave. In Western Australia, each year of continuous employment beyond the minimum qualifying tenure is worth one week of long service leave.
[36m(main_task pid=2937563)[0m </information>
