[36m(main_task pid=2937563)[0m Doc 5(Title: "") and are the recurring structural units of plakin-like proteins. These proteins are highly evolutionary conserved, from jellyfish to humans. D-Ala-(i)-D-serine-(i+2)-proline-(i+4) are the characteristic repeating residues of GOR repeats.[1] The main functional domain for GOR repeats is found within the acidic loop between strands 1 and 2 of the β-barrel region.[2] GOR repeats are named after Glycine-Ornithine-Arginine. Note that the amino acid serine is not found in human copies of GOR repeats; its position is usually replaced by a proline (D-Pipine), and the presence of serine is found only in some bacterial types.
